      "rects": []
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "image": "/slides/ids_ids_ids_5_ids_5_svm_pdf/slide_006.webp",
      "thumb": "/slides/ids_ids_ids_5_ids_5_svm_pdf/thumb_006.webp",
      "text": "Example: Understanding SVM using Cats and Dogs\r\n1. Suppose we want to develop a machine learning model that can identify whether an animal is a cat or a\r\ndog based on its features.\r\n2. First, we train the model using many labeled images of cats and dogs so that it can learn the\r\ndifferences between the two classes.\r\n3. SVM finds the best decision boundary to separate the cat and dog classes. The data points closest to\r\nthis boundary are called support vectors, which help determine the position of the boundary.\r\n4. When we provide a new image of an animal, the model uses the learned decision boundary to classify\r\nit as either a cat or a dog.\r\nExample output: The model classifies the new animal as a Cat.",
      "width": 1584,
      "height": 1224,
      "rects": [
        {
          "l": 1.76,
          "t": 51.17,
          "w": 47.34,
          "h": 2.32,
          "text": "Example: Understanding SVM using Cats and Dogs"
        },
        {
          "l": 1.79,
          "t": 54.41,
          "w": 93.6,
          "h": 2.32,
          "text": "1. Suppose we want to develop a machine learning model that can identify whether an animal is a cat or a"
        },
        {
          "l": 1.7,
          "t": 57.66,
          "w": 22.8,
          "h": 2.31,
          "text": "dog based on its features."
        },
        {
          "l": 1.68,
          "t": 60.89,
          "w": 93.76,
          "h": 2.31,
          "text": "2. First, we train the model using many labeled images of cats and dogs so that it can learn the"
        },
        {
          "l": 1.7,
          "t": 64.13,
          "w": 32.98,
          "h": 1.83,
          "text": "differences between the two classes."
        },
        {
          "l": 1.69,
          "t": 67.35,
          "w": 93.74,
          "h": 2.32,
          "text": "3. SVM finds the best decision boundary to separate the cat and dog classes. The data points closest to"
        },
        {
          "l": 1.62,
          "t": 70.6,
          "w": 82.42,
          "h": 2.28,
          "text": "this boundary are called support vectors, which help determine the position of the boundary."
        },
        {
          "l": 1.65,
          "t": 73.83,
          "w": 93.89,
          "h": 2.31,
          "text": "4. When we provide a new image of an animal, the model uses the learned decision boundary to classify"
        },
        {
          "l": 1.73,
          "t": 77.07,
          "w": 22.05,
          "h": 2.31,
          "text": "it as either a cat or a dog."
        },
        {
          "l": 1.77,
          "t": 80.29,
          "w": 55.64,
          "h": 2.3,
          "text": "Example output: The model classifies the new animal as a Cat."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "index": 7,
      "image": "/slides/ids_ids_ids_5_ids_5_svm_pdf/slide_007.webp",
      "thumb": "/slides/ids_ids_ids_5_ids_5_svm_pdf/thumb_007.webp",
      "text": "Types of SVM\r\nSVM can be of two types:\r\n1. Linear SVM: Linear SVM is used for linearly separable data, which means if a dataset can\r\nbe classified into two classes by using a single straight line, then such data is termed as\r\nlinearly separable data, and classifier used is called as Linear SVM classifier.\r\n2. Non-linear SVM: Non-Linear SVM is used for non-linearly separated data, which means if\r\na dataset cannot be classified by using a straight line, then such data is termed as non-linear\r\ndata and classifier used is called as Non-linear SVM classifier.",
      "width": 1584,
      "height": 1224,
      "rects": [
        {
          "l": 3.2,
          "t": 20.17,
          "w": 20.78,
          "h": 4.23,
